<DocsLayout title="Terms of Use — Querial">
    <div class="space-y-8">
        <div>
            <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold">Terms of Use</h1>
            <p class="text-muted-foreground mt-1">Last updated: August 2026</p>
        </div>
        <div class="prose dark:prose-invert max-w-none space-y-6 [&>p]:text-muted-foreground [&>h2]:text-foreground">
            <h2>1. Acceptance</h2>
            <p>
                These Terms govern your use of this documentation site and of Querial
                software as provided to you by the organization that hosts an instance
                (the “Instance Operator”). By accessing the site or using an instance,
                you agree to these Terms. If you do not agree, do not use them. For data
                handling, see the
                <a href="/docs/privacy" class="text-link hover:underline">Privacy Policy</a>.
            </p>

            <h2>2. What Querial is</h2>
            <p>
                Querial is a self-hosted, SQL-native control plane for designing,
                versioning, deploying, and operating database pipelines against
                PostgreSQL, SQL Server, and MySQL targets. It is not a hosted SaaS
                operated from this website, a replacement database, a no-code designer,
                or a general-purpose workflow engine.
            </p>

            <h2>3. Your content and SQL</h2>
            <p>
                Pipeline SQL, parameters, artifacts, and target data remain the
                responsibility of the Instance Operator and of the users they invite.
                You must not place secrets in packages, Git trees, logs, or SqlAssist
                prompts. Published versions are immutable; operators deploy versions,
                not ad-hoc edits on production.
            </p>

            <h2>4. Accounts and roles</h2>
            <p>
                Access is granted by the Instance Operator through Setup, invite, or
                user create. Pending invitees cannot sign in until they set a password.
                Roles (Developer, DevOps, Project admin, Team admin, Platform admin)
                limit what you can design, operate, or administer. Circumventing those
                gates — including forged cookies or keys from another project — is
                prohibited.
            </p>

            <h2>5. Disclaimer</h2>
            <p>
                Software and documentation are provided as-is, without warranty of
                merchantability or fitness for a particular purpose, to the extent
                permitted by law. Pipeline execution can change data on target
                databases you configure. Bounded SQL preview is not a production run.
            </p>

            <h2>6. Changes</h2>
            <p>
                The Instance Operator may update software and these Terms. Continued
                use after notice constitutes acceptance of the updated Terms.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
</DocsLayout>
